### What is the Collections utility class?

The `java.util.Collections` class is a utility class that provides static methods to operate on or return collections. It was introduced alongside the Collections Framework in JDK 1.2 and has been expanded in every major release since.

The class is final and has a private constructor, so you can never create an instance. You always call its methods using the class name: `Collections.sort(list)`, `Collections.unmodifiableMap(map)`.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class CollectionsClassDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Collections is a utility class – all static
        // new Collections(); // compile error – private constructor

        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of("CUR3001", "SAV2001", "FD4001"));
        Collections.sort(accounts);
        System.out.println("Sorted: " + accounts);
    }
}
```

```text
Sorted: [CUR3001, FD4001, SAV2001]
```

### How does sorting work?

The `Collections.sort()` method sorts a list in place. It uses TimSort (a hybrid of merge sort and insertion sort) which is stable – equal elements keep their relative order.

You can sort in three ways:

- **Natural order**: `Collections.sort(list)` – elements must implement `Comparable`.
- **Custom comparator**: `Collections.sort(list, comparator)` – you provide the ordering logic.
- **List.sort()**: Since Java 8, you can call `list.sort(comparator)` directly. Under the hood it delegates to the same TimSort.

The time complexity is O(n log n) in the average and worst case.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class SortingDemo {
    record Account(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
            new Account("CUR3001", "Priya", new BigDecimal("75000")),
            new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("150000")),
            new Account("FD4001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("500000"))
        ));

        // Sort by balance ascending
        Collections.sort(accounts, Comparator.comparing(Account::balance));
        accounts.forEach(a -> System.out.println(a.id() + ": " + a.balance()));
        System.out.println();

        // Sort by holder name, then by balance descending
        accounts.sort(
            Comparator.comparing(Account::holder)
                      .thenComparing(Account::balance, Comparator.reverseOrder())
        );
        accounts.forEach(a ->
            System.out.println(a.holder() + " | " + a.id() + ": " + a.balance()));
        System.out.println();

        // Reverse order of strings
        List<String> ids = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001"));
        Collections.sort(ids, Comparator.reverseOrder());
        System.out.println("Reverse: " + ids);
    }
}
```

```text
CUR3001: 75000
SAV2001: 150000
FD4001: 500000

Priya | CUR3001: 75000
Rajesh | FD4001: 500000
Rajesh | SAV2001: 150000

Reverse: [SAV2001, FD4001, CUR3001]
```

### How does binary search work?

`Collections.binarySearch(list, key)` finds an element in O(log n) time using the binary search algorithm. The list **must be sorted** before you call binarySearch, or the result is undefined – it will not throw an exception, it will just return a wrong answer.

If the element is found, binarySearch returns its index. If not found, it returns `-(insertion point) - 1`, where insertion point is the index at which the element would be inserted to keep the list sorted.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class BinarySearchDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
            "CUR3001", "FD4001", "SAV2001", "SAV2002", "SAV2003"
        ));
        // List is already sorted – required for binarySearch

        int index = Collections.binarySearch(accounts, "FD4001");
        System.out.println("Found FD4001 at index: " + index);

        int notFound = Collections.binarySearch(accounts, "FD9999");
        System.out.println("FD9999 result: " + notFound);
        // Negative means not found. Insertion point = -(-2) - 1 = 1
        int insertionPoint = -(notFound + 1);
        System.out.println("Would insert at: " + insertionPoint);

        // WRONG – searching unsorted list gives WRONG result silently
        List<String> unsorted = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001");
        int wrongResult = Collections.binarySearch(unsorted, "FD4001");
        System.out.println("Unsorted search (unreliable): " + wrongResult);
    }
}
```

```text
Found FD4001 at index: 1
FD9999 result: -2
Would insert at: 1
Unsorted search (unreliable): -1
```

### How do min, max, and frequency work?

`Collections.min()` and `Collections.max()` find the smallest and largest elements in a collection. They accept either natural ordering or a custom comparator. `Collections.frequency()` counts how many times an element appears.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class MinMaxFrequencyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<BigDecimal> transactions = List.of(
            new BigDecimal("50000"),
            new BigDecimal("10000"),
            new BigDecimal("75000"),
            new BigDecimal("10000"),
            new BigDecimal("25000")
        );

        BigDecimal min = Collections.min(transactions);
        BigDecimal max = Collections.max(transactions);
        System.out.println("Smallest transaction: " + min);
        System.out.println("Largest transaction: " + max);

        // Frequency – count occurrences
        int count = Collections.frequency(transactions, new BigDecimal("10000"));
        System.out.println("Transactions of 10000: " + count);

        // Min/Max with custom comparator
        List<String> accountIds = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001");
        String shortest = Collections.min(accountIds, Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));
        System.out.println("Shortest ID: " + shortest);

        // Empty collection – throws NoSuchElementException
        try {
            Collections.min(Collections.emptyList());
        } catch (java.util.NoSuchElementException e) {
            System.out.println("Empty collection: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Smallest transaction: 10000
Largest transaction: 75000
Transactions of 10000: 2
Shortest ID: FD4001
Empty collection: NoSuchElementException
```

### How do reordering methods work?

The Collections class provides four methods for changing element positions in a list.

- **`reverse(list)`** – reverses the order of elements in place.
- **`shuffle(list)`** – randomly permutes the elements. Accepts an optional `Random` for reproducible shuffles.
- **`rotate(list, distance)`** – rotates elements by the specified distance. Positive rotates right, negative rotates left.
- **`swap(list, i, j)`** – swaps the elements at positions i and j.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class ReorderingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> queue = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
            "Rajesh", "Priya", "Amit", "Sneha", "Vikram"
        ));
        System.out.println("Original: " + queue);

        // Reverse – flip the queue
        Collections.reverse(queue);
        System.out.println("Reversed: " + queue);

        // Shuffle – random reorder (e.g., lottery draw)
        Collections.shuffle(queue, new Random(42)); // seed for reproducibility
        System.out.println("Shuffled: " + queue);

        // Rotate right by 2
        Collections.rotate(queue, 2);
        System.out.println("Rotated +2: " + queue);

        // Swap positions 0 and 4
        Collections.swap(queue, 0, 4);
        System.out.println("Swapped 0,4: " + queue);
    }
}
```

```text
Original: [Rajesh, Priya, Amit, Sneha, Vikram]
Reversed: [Vikram, Sneha, Amit, Priya, Rajesh]
Shuffled: [Amit, Priya, Vikram, Rajesh, Sneha]
Rotated +2: [Rajesh, Sneha, Amit, Priya, Vikram]
Swapped 0,4: [Vikram, Sneha, Amit, Priya, Rajesh]
```

### How do unmodifiable wrappers work?

`Collections.unmodifiableList()`, `unmodifiableSet()`, and `unmodifiableMap()` return read-only views of the original collection. Any attempt to modify the view (add, remove, set, put, clear) throws `UnsupportedOperationException`.

The critical gotcha: the wrapper is a **view**, not a copy. If the original collection changes, the "unmodifiable" view reflects those changes. This is a common source of bugs.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class UnmodifiableDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> original = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001"));
        List<String> readOnly = Collections.unmodifiableList(original);

        // Cannot modify through the view
        try {
            readOnly.add("FD4001");
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.println("Cannot add to view: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        // But the original can still be modified!
        original.add("FD4001");
        System.out.println("View after original change: " + readOnly);
        // The view REFLECTS the change – it is not immutable!

        // For TRUE immutability:
        // Java 9:  List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001")
        // Java 10: List.copyOf(original) – makes a copy
        List<String> trulyImmutable = List.copyOf(original);
        original.add("LON5001");
        System.out.println("Original: " + original);
        System.out.println("True copy: " + trulyImmutable); // unchanged
    }
}
```

```text
Cannot add to view: UnsupportedOperationException
View after original change: [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001]
Original: [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001, LON5001]
True copy: [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001]
```

### How do synchronized wrappers work?

`Collections.synchronizedList()`, `synchronizedMap()`, and `synchronizedSet()` wrap every method in a `synchronized` block, making the collection thread-safe for individual operations.

The trap: iteration is NOT automatically synchronized. You must wrap the entire iteration in a `synchronized` block yourself, or you will get `ConcurrentModificationException`.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class SynchronizedWrapperDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, String> map =
            Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());
        map.put("SAV2001", "Rajesh");
        map.put("CUR3001", "Priya");

        // Individual operations are thread-safe
        String holder = map.get("SAV2001"); // synchronized internally

        // BUT iteration requires external synchronization
        synchronized (map) {
            for (Map.Entry<String, String> entry : map.entrySet()) {
                System.out.println(entry.getKey() + " -> " + entry.getValue());
            }
        }

        // BETTER ALTERNATIVE: ConcurrentHashMap
        // No external sync needed, finer-grained locking, much faster
        java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> concurrent =
            new java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        concurrent.put("SAV2001", "Rajesh");
        concurrent.forEach((k, v) -> System.out.println(k + " -> " + v));
    }
}
```

```text
SAV2001 -> Rajesh
CUR3001 -> Priya
SAV2001 -> Rajesh
```

### How do checked wrappers work?

`Collections.checkedList()`, `checkedMap()`, and `checkedSet()` add runtime type checking. They are useful for debugging heap pollution caused by raw types or unchecked casts.

Without a checked wrapper, a raw-type list can accept the wrong type, and the error only surfaces later when you try to use the element. A checked wrapper catches the error at the point of insertion.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class CheckedWrapperDemo {
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Without checked wrapper – pollution goes undetected
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>();
        List raw = accounts; // raw type – no generic checks
        raw.add(42); // no error here!
        // accounts.get(0).toUpperCase(); // ClassCastException here – too late!

        // With checked wrapper – catches at insertion time
        List<String> checked = Collections.checkedList(new ArrayList<>(), String.class);
        List rawChecked = checked;
        try {
            rawChecked.add(42); // ClassCastException immediately!
        } catch (ClassCastException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught at insertion: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Caught at insertion: Attempt to insert class java.lang.Integer element into collection with element type class java.lang.String
```

### What are the factory methods?

`Collections` provides factory methods for creating special-purpose collections.

- **`emptyList()`**, **`emptySet()`**, **`emptyMap()`** – return immutable empty collections. Useful as default return values to avoid null.
- **`singletonList(element)`**, **`singleton(element)`**, **`singletonMap(key, value)`** – return immutable collections with exactly one element.
- **`nCopies(n, element)`** – returns an immutable list with n copies of the same element. The element is not actually duplicated in memory – the list returns the same reference for every index.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class FactoryMethodsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // emptyList – better than returning null
        List<String> noAccounts = Collections.emptyList();
        System.out.println("Empty: " + noAccounts.size());

        // singletonList – immutable, one element
        List<String> oneAccount = Collections.singletonList("SAV2001");
        System.out.println("Singleton: " + oneAccount);

        // nCopies – n copies of the same element
        List<String> defaults = Collections.nCopies(5, "PENDING");
        System.out.println("Copies: " + defaults);

        // All are immutable
        try {
            noAccounts.add("X");
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.println("Empty is immutable");
        }
        try {
            oneAccount.add("X");
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.println("Singleton is immutable");
        }
        try {
            defaults.set(0, "X");
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.println("nCopies is immutable");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: 0
Singleton: [SAV2001]
Copies: [PENDING, PENDING, PENDING, PENDING, PENDING]
Empty is immutable
Singleton is immutable
nCopies is immutable
```

### What are common mistakes with the Collections class?

#### Mistake 1: Calling binarySearch on an unsorted list

```java
List<String> unsorted = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001"));
// WRONG – list is not sorted, result is undefined
int idx = Collections.binarySearch(unsorted, "CUR3001"); // may return wrong index

// CORRECT – sort first
Collections.sort(unsorted);
int correctIdx = Collections.binarySearch(unsorted, "CUR3001"); // 0
```

#### Mistake 2: Assuming unmodifiable means immutable

```java
List<String> original = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001"));
List<String> readOnly = Collections.unmodifiableList(original);
original.add("CUR3001"); // original changes
System.out.println(readOnly); // [SAV2001, CUR3001] – view reflects change!

// CORRECT – use List.copyOf() for true immutability
List<String> trulyImmutable = List.copyOf(original);
```

#### Mistake 3: Iterating a synchronized collection without external sync

```java
List<String> syncList = Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>());
// WRONG – no synchronization during iteration
for (String s : syncList) { } // ConcurrentModificationException risk

// CORRECT – wrap iteration in synchronized block
synchronized (syncList) {
    for (String s : syncList) { }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **TimSort internals.** `Collections.sort()` uses TimSort (since Java 7), which is O(n log n) worst case and O(n) best case when the data is partially sorted. It detects existing sorted "runs" in the data and merges them. This makes it fast for nearly-sorted data, which is common in banking (transactions often arrive in roughly chronological order).

- **sort() vs parallelSort().** `Collections.sort()` is single-threaded. For very large lists (millions of elements), `Arrays.parallelSort()` uses the ForkJoinPool to sort in parallel. There is no `Collections.parallelSort()` – you must convert to an array first.

- **unmodifiableList is shallow.** If your list contains mutable objects, the objects themselves can still be modified through the unmodifiable view. Only the list structure is protected. For deep immutability, make the contained objects immutable too (use records or final fields).

- **Collections.singletonList vs List.of.** Both create immutable single-element lists. `singletonList` allows null; `List.of` does not. Prefer `List.of` in new code because it is null-hostile, which catches bugs earlier.

- **Performance of synchronized wrappers.** Every method acquires the same mutex lock. Under contention (multiple threads), this creates a bottleneck. `ConcurrentHashMap` uses bucket-level locking (or CAS) and achieves much higher throughput. Use synchronized wrappers only when you have no alternative.

- **The disjoint() method.** `Collections.disjoint(c1, c2)` returns true if the two collections have no elements in common. Useful for checking if a customer's account set overlaps with a blacklist.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the difference between Collections.unmodifiableList and List.of?" – view vs copy, null handling, Java version.
- "What is the difference between Collections.synchronizedMap and ConcurrentHashMap?" – single lock vs segmented locks, iteration behavior.
- "Can you sort a list of custom objects?" – yes, implement Comparable or provide a Comparator.
- "What happens if you call binarySearch on an unsorted list?" – undefined result, no exception.
- "How does TimSort work?" – finds runs, merges them, O(n) best case for partially sorted data.
